Find common elements between two lists
Problem statement

Find the elements present in both lists.

Input:
List<Integer> list1 = Arrays.asList(1, 2, 3, 4, 5);

List<Integer> list2 = Arrays.asList(3, 4, 5, 6, 7);


Expected output:
[3, 4, 5]


A. Approach and logic
We need to identify which elements from the first list are also present in the second list.

A simple approach:
1. Iterate through the first list.
2. Check whether the second list contains that element.
3. If yes, add it to the result.
4. Avoid adding duplicate elements.
B. Plain Java – Optimized using HashSet
List<Integer> list1 = Arrays.asList(1, 2, 3, 4, 5);

List<Integer> list2 = Arrays.asList(3, 4, 5, 6, 7);

Set<Integer> set = new HashSet<>(list2);

Set<Integer> common = new LinkedHashSet<>();

for (Integer num : list1) {

    if (set.contains(num)) {
        common.add(num);
    }
}

System.out.println(common);


C. Java 8
List<Integer> list1 = Arrays.asList(1, 2, 3, 4, 5);

List<Integer> list2 = Arrays.asList(3, 4, 5, 6, 7);

Set<Integer> set = new HashSet<>(list2);

List<Integer> common = list1.stream()
        .filter(set::contains)
        .distinct()
        .collect(Collectors.toList());

System.out.println(common);


D. Complexity
Let n be the size of the first list and m be the size of the second list.
Solution	Time	Extra space
Nested/list contains approach	O(n × m)	O(n)
HashSet approach	O(n + m) average	O(m + n)
Java 8 with HashSet	O(n + m) average	O(m + n)

E. Choice of solution
Use HashSet for large lists.

Why?

ArrayList.contains() takes O(n) in the worst case, while HashSet.contains() takes O(1) average time.

This is a good example to demonstrate that selecting the right data structure can significantly improve performance.
